x(t)&=\cos{\left(t \right)},\qquad y(t)=4 \sin{\left(t \right)},\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- \cos^{2}{\left(t \right)} + 2 \cos{\left(t \right)},\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=8 \sin{\left(t \right)} + 1,\\x\prime(t)&=- \sin{\left(t \right)},\qquad y\prime(t)=4 \cos{\left(t \right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:

$$
\begin{aligned}
P y\prime-Q x\prime&=\left(- \cos^{2}{\left(t \right)} + 2 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\qquad\cdot\left(4 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\quad-\left(8 \sin{\left(t \right)} + 1\right)\\&\qquad\cdot\left(- \sin{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&=8 \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}+\sin{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}-4 \cos^{3}{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}+8 \cos^{2}{\left(t \right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Using $\cos^2t+\sin^2t=1$, simplify the integrand:

$$
\begin{aligned}
P y\prime-Q x\prime&=\sin{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}-4 \cos^{3}{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}+8.
\end{aligned}
$$

For the other powers, use $\cos^3t=(1-\sin^2t)\cos t$.

For terms containing a remaining factor $\cos t$, use $u=\sin t$, $\mathrm{d}u=\cos t\,\mathrm{d}t$.

Integrate these terms separately:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int - 4 \cos^{3}{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=\frac{4 \sin^{3}{\left(t \right)}}{3} - 4 \sin{\left(t \right)}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int \sin{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=- \cos{\left(t \right)}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int 8\,\mathrm{d}t&=8 t+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

For the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[\frac{4 \sin^{3}{\left(t \right)}}{3} - 4 \sin{\left(t \right)}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=0\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[- \cos{\left(t \right)}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=-1\\&\quad-\left(-1\right)\\&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[8 t\right]_0^{2\pi}&=16 \pi\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=16 \pi.
\end{aligned}
$$

Adding the contributions gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi&=(0) + (0) + (16 \pi)=16 \pi.
\end{aligned}
$$

The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi&=\oint_C P\,\mathrm{d}y-Q\,\mathrm{d}x\\&=\int_0^{2\pi}(P y\prime-Q x\prime)\,\mathrm{d}t.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x(t)&=\cos{\left(t \right)},\qquad y(t)=2 \sin{\left(t \right)},\\P(x(t),y(t))&=\cos^{3}{\left(t \right)},\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=- 6 \sin{\left(t \right)} - 2 \cos{\left(t \right)},\\x\prime(t)&=- \sin{\left(t \right)},\qquad y\prime(t)=2 \cos{\left(t \right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:

$$
\begin{aligned}
P y\prime-Q x\prime&=\left(\cos^{3}{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\qquad\cdot\left(2 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\quad-\left(- 6 \sin{\left(t \right)} - 2 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\qquad\cdot\left(- \sin{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&=- 6 \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}-2 \sin{\left(t \right)} \cos{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}+2 \cos^{4}{\left(t \right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Reduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\cos^4t&=\left(\frac{1+\cos(2t)}2\right)^2\\&=\frac14\left(1+2\cos(2t)+\frac{1+\cos(4t)}2\right)\\&=\frac{3+4\cos(2t)+\cos(4t)}8.
\end{aligned}
$$

For the other powers, use $\sin^2t=\frac{1-\cos(2t)}2$.

For terms containing a remaining factor $\cos t$, use $u=\sin t$, $\mathrm{d}u=\cos t\,\mathrm{d}t$.

Integrate these terms separately:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int 2 \cos^{4}{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=\frac{3 t}{4} + \frac{\sin{\left(2 t \right)}}{2} + \frac{\sin{\left(4 t \right)}}{16}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int - 2 \sin{\left(t \right)} \cos{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=- \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int - 6 \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=- 3 t + \frac{3 \sin{\left(2 t \right)}}{2}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

For the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[\frac{3 t}{4} + \frac{\sin{\left(2 t \right)}}{2} + \frac{\sin{\left(4 t \right)}}{16}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=\frac{3 \pi}{2}\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=\frac{3 \pi}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[- \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=0\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[- 3 t + \frac{3 \sin{\left(2 t \right)}}{2}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=- 6 \pi\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=- 6 \pi.
\end{aligned}
$$

Adding the contributions gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi&=\left(\frac{3 \pi}{2}\right) + (0) + (- 6 \pi)=- \frac{9 \pi}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi&=\oint_C P\,\mathrm{d}y-Q\,\mathrm{d}x\\&=\int_0^{2\pi}(P y\prime-Q x\prime)\,\mathrm{d}t.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x(t)&=2 \cos{\left(t \right)},\qquad y(t)=2 \sin{\left(t \right)},\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- 2 \sin{\left(t \right)},\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=8 \sin^{3}{\left(t \right)},\\x\prime(t)&=- 2 \sin{\left(t \right)},\qquad y\prime(t)=2 \cos{\left(t \right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:

$$
\begin{aligned}
P y\prime-Q x\prime&=\left(- 2 \sin{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\qquad\cdot\left(2 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\quad-\left(8 \sin^{3}{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\qquad\cdot\left(- 2 \sin{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&=16 \sin^{4}{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}-4 \sin{\left(t \right)} \cos{\left(t \right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Reduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\sin^4t&=\left(\frac{1-\cos(2t)}2\right)^2\\&=\frac14\left(1-2\cos(2t)+\frac{1+\cos(4t)}2\right)\\&=\frac{3-4\cos(2t)+\cos(4t)}8.
\end{aligned}
$$

For terms containing a remaining factor $\cos t$, use $u=\sin t$, $\mathrm{d}u=\cos t\,\mathrm{d}t$.

Integrate these terms separately:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int - 4 \sin{\left(t \right)} \cos{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=- 2 \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int 16 \sin^{4}{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=6 t - 4 \sin{\left(2 t \right)} + \frac{\sin{\left(4 t \right)}}{2}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

For the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[- 2 \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=0\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[6 t - 4 \sin{\left(2 t \right)} + \frac{\sin{\left(4 t \right)}}{2}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=12 \pi\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=12 \pi.
\end{aligned}
$$

Adding the contributions gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi&=(0) + (12 \pi)=12 \pi.
\end{aligned}
$$

The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi&=\oint_C P\,\mathrm{d}y-Q\,\mathrm{d}x\\&=\int_0^{2\pi}(P y\prime-Q x\prime)\,\mathrm{d}t.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x(t)&=\cos{\left(t \right)},\qquad y(t)=\sin{\left(t \right)},\\P(x(t),y(t))&=\sin{\left(t \right)} - \cos^{3}{\left(t \right)},\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=3 \sin{\left(t \right)} + \cos{\left(t \right)},\\x\prime(t)&=- \sin{\left(t \right)},\qquad y\prime(t)=\cos{\left(t \right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:

$$
\begin{aligned}
P y\prime-Q x\prime&=\left(\sin{\left(t \right)} - \cos^{3}{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\qquad\cdot\left(\cos{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\quad-\left(3 \sin{\left(t \right)} + \cos{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\qquad\cdot\left(- \sin{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&=3 \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}+2 \sin{\left(t \right)} \cos{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}-\cos^{4}{\left(t \right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Reduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\cos^4t&=\left(\frac{1+\cos(2t)}2\right)^2\\&=\frac14\left(1+2\cos(2t)+\frac{1+\cos(4t)}2\right)\\&=\frac{3+4\cos(2t)+\cos(4t)}8.
\end{aligned}
$$

For the other powers, use $\sin^2t=\frac{1-\cos(2t)}2$.

For terms containing a remaining factor $\cos t$, use $u=\sin t$, $\mathrm{d}u=\cos t\,\mathrm{d}t$.

Integrate these terms separately:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int - \cos^{4}{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=- \frac{3 t}{8} - \frac{\sin{\left(2 t \right)}}{4} - \frac{\sin{\left(4 t \right)}}{32}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int 2 \sin{\left(t \right)} \cos{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=\sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int 3 \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=\frac{3 t}{2} - \frac{3 \sin{\left(2 t \right)}}{4}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

For the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[- \frac{3 t}{8} - \frac{\sin{\left(2 t \right)}}{4} - \frac{\sin{\left(4 t \right)}}{32}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=- \frac{3 \pi}{4}\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=- \frac{3 \pi}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[\sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=0\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[\frac{3 t}{2} - \frac{3 \sin{\left(2 t \right)}}{4}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=3 \pi\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=3 \pi.
\end{aligned}
$$

Adding the contributions gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi&=\left(- \frac{3 \pi}{4}\right) + (0) + (3 \pi)=\frac{9 \pi}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$
